{
  "id": "celery_celery_10493",
  "repo_name": "celery/celery",
  "repo_url": "https://github.com/celery/celery",
  "base_commit": "0690889a935aaae877a1c3ec8f3606fe712d0855",
  "pr_number": 10493,
  "issue_number": 10489,
  "issue_title": "Memory-leak fix (traceback_clear) silently fails in Request.on_timeout()",
  "issue_body": "# Checklist\n\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] This has already been asked to the discussions forum first.\n- [x] I have read the relevant section in the contribution guide on reporting bugs.\n- [x] I have checked the issues list for similar or identical bug reports.\n- [x] I have checked the pull requests list for existing proposed fixes.\n- [x] I have checked the commit log to find out if the bug was already fixed in the main branch.\n- [x] I have included all related issues and possible duplicate issues in this issue (If there are none, check this box anyway).\n- [x] I have tried to reproduce the issue with pytest-celery and added the reproduction script below.\n\n## Mandatory Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have included the output of ``celery -A proj report`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have verified that the issue exists against the `main` branch of Celery.\n- [x] I have included the contents of ``pip freeze`` in the issue.\n- [x] I have included all the versions of all the external dependencies required to reproduce this bug.\n\n## Optional Debugging Information\n\n- [x] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one Python version and/or implementation.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one message broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one version of the message broker and/or result backend.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one operating system.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue on more than one workers pool.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue with autoscaling, retries, ETA/Countdown & rate limits disabled.\n- [ ] I have tried reproducing the issue after downgrading and/or upgrading Celery and its dependencies.\n\n## Related Issues and Possible Duplicates\n\n#### Related Issues\n\n- The `traceback_clear()` helper this bug concerns was introduced in `celery/app/trace.py` as a memory-leak fix (referenced in code as issue #8882). This report concerns a separate call site of that same helper, added later in `celery/worker/request.py`'s `Request.on_timeout()`, which does not exhibit the same behavior as the original fix.\n\n#### Possible Duplicates\n\n- None\n\n## Environment & Settings\n\n**Celery version**: 5.6.2 (`main` branch, as of this report)\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>celery report</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\nsoftware -> celery:5.6.2 (main) kombu:5.x py:3.12.x\nbilliard:4.x\nplatform -> system:Linux\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n# Steps to Reproduce\n\n## Required Dependencies\n\n- **Minimal Python Version**: Any (reproduced on 3.12; not version-specific \u2014 behavior is a documented CPython frame API restriction)\n- **Minimal Celery Version**: 5.6.x (`main`) \u2014 anywhere `traceback_clear()` is called from `Request.on_timeout()`\n- **Minimal Kombu Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal OS and/or Kernel Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Broker Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n- **Minimal Result Backend Client Version**: N/A or Unknown\n\n### Python Packages\n\n<details>\n<summary><b><code>pip freeze</code> Output:</b></summary>\n<p>\n\n```\ncelery==5.6.2\nbilliard==4.x\nkombu==5.x\nvine==5.x\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\n### Other Dependencies\n\n<details>\n<p>\nN/A \u2014 this is a pure-Python/CPython behavior issue, not dependent on broker, backend, or worker pool.\n</p>\n</details>\n\n## Minimally Reproducible Test Case\n\nThe following script reproduces the exact structure used in `Request.on_timeout()`'s hard-time-limit branch (`celery/worker/request.py`) and demonstrates that `traceback_clear()` (`celery/app/trace.py`) never succeeds in clearing any frame when called this way, because the only frame in the synthetic traceback is the currently-executing frame itself, which CPython refuses to clear.\n\n<details>\n<p>\n\n```python\n\"\"\"\nReproduces the exact pattern used in celery.worker.request.Request.on_timeout()\nfor the hard time-limit branch:\n\n    try:\n        try:\n            raise exc\n        except TimeLimitExceeded:\n            einfo = ExceptionInfo()\n        ...\n        traceback_clear(exc)   # <-- intended to free frame locals\n    finally:\n        exc.__traceback__ = None\n\nThis shows traceback_clear() can never actually clear a frame in this call site.\n\"\"\"\n\nfrom celery.app.trace import traceback_clear\n\n\ndef simulate_on_timeout():\n    exc = ValueError(\"TimeLimitExceeded (simulated)\")\n\n    try:\n        try:\n            raise exc\n        except ValueError:\n            pass  # equivalent to einfo = ExceptionInfo()\n\n        # This mirrors the real call: traceback_clear(exc)\n        # Patch tb_frame.clear to observe whether it ever succeeds.\n        tb = exc.__traceback__\n        cleared_any = False\n        while tb is not None:\n            try:\n                tb.tb_frame.clear()\n                cleared_any = True\n            except RuntimeError as e:\n                print(f\"frame.clear() raised (swallowed in real code): {e}\")\n            tb = tb.tb_next\n\n        assert cleared_any is False, (\n            \"Expected traceback_clear() to be a no-op here, but a frame was cleared.\"\n        )\n        print(\"Confirmed: traceback_clear() cleared ZERO frames.\")\n    finally:\n        exc.__traceback__ = None\n\n\nsimulate_on_timeout()\n```\n\n</p>\n</details>\n\nActual `celery/app/trace.py::traceback_clear` swallows the `RuntimeError` internally (`except RuntimeError: pass`), so in production this failure is completely silent \u2014 no log line, no error, nothing. It can only be observed by instrumenting the function directly, as above.\n\n# Expected Behavior\n\nThe comment above the `traceback_clear(exc)` call in `Request.on_timeout()` states:\n\n```python\n# MEMORY LEAK FIX: clear frame locals retained by the\n# synthetic traceback (same pattern as trace.py #8882).\ntraceback_clear(exc)\n```\n\nThis implies the call clears frame-local references (e.g. to `self`, the `Request` object, and other locals held by the frame) from the traceback attached to `exc`, mirroring the effective leak fix already in place in `celery/app/trace.py`'s main tracer. This should measurably release references to large/circular objects held by that frame.\n\n# Actual Behavior\n\n`traceback_clear(exc)` never clears anything in this call site, on every single invocation.\n\n**Root cause:** In `Request.on_timeout()`, `exc` is raised and caught within the same function (`raise exc` / `except TimeLimitExceeded:`), producing a synthetic, single-frame traceback whose only frame is `on_timeout()`'s own frame. Because `on_timeout()` has not yet returned when `traceback_clear(exc)` is called, that frame is still \"executing\" from CPython's point of view. `frame.clear()` unconditionally raises:\n\n```\nRuntimeError: cannot clear an executing frame\n```\n\nfor any frame still on the call stack. `traceback_clear()` catches this `RuntimeError` and silently ignores it (`except RuntimeError: pass`), so the call appears to succeed but performs no work.\n\nThis differs from the original leak fix in `celery/app/trace.py` (issue #8882), where `traceback_clear()` is invoked from a context where the traceback includes frames from the task body that have already unwound (are no longer executing), so `frame.clear()` can succeed there.\n\n**Practical impact:**\n- The call is dead code for this specific call site \u2014 it always fails and always silently swallows the failure, on every hard-timeout event.\n- It does real (if small) unnecessary work (walking the traceback chain, attempting and catching a `RuntimeError`) on a code path that already runs during worker-level failure handling.\n- The comment is misleading to future maintainers/reviewers, who would reasonably believe this line is functioning as a memory-leak mitigation when it structurally cannot.\n- Separately, `exc.__traceback__ = None` in the enclosing `finally` block does appear to correctly break the `frame \u2192 exc \u2192 traceback \u2192 frame` reference cycle on its own, so there is likely no live leak today \u2014 but that means `traceback_clear(exc)` is fully redundant here, and if that `finally` line is ever refactored or removed independently (a realistic risk given the misleading comment implies `traceback_clear` is the actual fix), the cycle-breaking behavior would silently regress with no test coverage catching it.\n\n**Suggested fix:** either remove the redundant `traceback_clear(exc)` call at this call site (since `exc.__traceback__ = None` already accomplishes the intended reference-breaking), or restructure so the traceback-frame clearing happens only after `on_timeout()`'s own frame is no longer on the call stack (e.g. clear only `tb.tb_next` frames onward, skipping the frame that raised `exc`).",
  "merged_at": "2026-08-18T14:22:18Z",
